foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Thursday November 10th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  Mastodon a decentralized microblogging  site has been blowing up in popularity  since Elon Musk completed his purchase  of Twitter so what's it like to use the  Twitter alternative and what makes  Mastodon so appealing our senior  personal Tech columnist Joanna Stern has  been giving it a go and she's here to  tell us all about it hi Joanna thanks  for coming on the show anytime so if  people are switching from Twitter to  Mastodon I mean how familiar will  Mastodon seem to people who do use  Twitter  eventually really simple but there's a  big area from getting from signing up to  figuring this out that is really  complicated I like to say it's harder to  explain than to my child why he can't  have Sour Patch Kids for every meal I  mean it's like some of it just seems  impossible but you can get through it  all and when you do get through it all  you'll have an experience that's similar  to Twitter  so it's like a a micro blogging platform  as well with where I'll have like a  username and be able to write you know  whatever I want to the world yeah so to  understand it you have to take a step  back and really what this is is  something called decentralized social  media or Federated social media as some  are calling it and so instead of one big  Tech Giant like meta with Facebook and  Instagram or Twitter with Elon Musk this  type of social network is distributed  and so the idea is is that anyone can  really run parts of this social network  and it's operated by different servers  and all of these different servers make  up the bigger social network and I know  it's starting to sound confusing but  what's nice about this is that it isn't  in the hands of one Central person and  that means that users and the people who  are running these servers can make up  the rules and they make sure sure things  are running smoothly okay so if it's not  run by one big company or one you know  individual like Elon Musk what does that  mean for people who are signing up for  Mastodon what will it mean for using the  platform yes so signing up for the  platform is definitely more complicated  than signing up for a Twitter account or  a Facebook account  you have to first sign up for a server  because as I mentioned this is all about  different servers working together and  talking to each other for this bigger  social media platform which is called  Mastodon so all these servers they make  up the Mastodon platform so when you  first sign up you select a server and  then you select a username and similar  to email you kind of get an address  together right so my server that I  signed up for last week is  mastodon.world and my username is Joanna  Stern and so that makes my whole address  Joanna Stern at mastodon.world  and so that server is where my  information my profile my posts my  database of followers and the people I  follow that's all housed on that  mastodon.world server okay so how do you  go about picking a server is there a  method to this  yeah so and that's really the hardest  part of the sign up process is picking  this server I mean first for normal  people they're like why do I need to  pick a server what is this say I'm not  an I.T consultant right so first you're  like gotta understand what this thing is  second you want to pick a server that  makes sense and so one of the ways that  the servers are sort of classified is  based on different interests you'll see  some organized by artists or music and  different interests lgbtq journalists  Etc I want to say something though that  is very important here it doesn't matter  what server you're on you can talk to  any person on any server so even though  I'm on mastodon.world and say Zoe you  are on  macedon.social I would still be able to  see your posts if I followed you and you  would still be able to see my posts if  you followed me you know you might pick  a server you said because you have a  group or Community but you know who are  the people behind them if they're not  say a company and how do you know you  can trust them with your information  yeah so this was the biggest question I  set out to find out with this piece  because I signed up for this  mastodon.world server and I started  understanding how this service works  right okay some private server owned by  somebody is housing this information and  happens to be some of my personal  information right it's got my profile  it's got a photo of me it's got my posts  even got direct messages that I'm  putting on the server so I decided to  get in touch with the person who runs  This Server and he happens to live in  the Netherlands and he decided to start  doing this as a hobby a couple of years  ago and so I got in touch with him and I  asked him okay what happens if your  server goes down he says well if my  server goes down you know yeah you would  lose everything but I've got a backup  for my server I said okay well what  about the messages that I'm sending on  direct messages can you see them he's  like well I could see them if I wanted  to if I went into gigabytes of data but  I don't want to and I don't plan to do  that unless there's some real extreme  circumstance to do that  so the conversation really to me was  about how much trust we're putting into  these people that operate the servers  and truthfully that's the case for any  social media platform you know the fact  that Mastodon is decentralized and  that's where so many people are headed  doesn't tell us anything you know sort  of overall about what individuals are  looking for when they go on social media  I think right now there is definitely a  group of people who have been on Twitter  for a long time and now are seeing the  service really disrupted by Elon Musk  For Better or For Worse we don't know  yet but there are those that are feeling  right now like this is not the place  that I came to 10 years ago when I was  first starting to use Twitter or social  media and those people might be longing  for something else I definitely know  that you know having been on Mastodon  over the last couple of days there is a  different sense of community there's a  different sense of people actually just  wanting to talk with each other and find  information versus Twitter right now  which is really just riled up in the new  ownership and lots of talk about the  principles of free speech and blue check  marks all right that's our senior  personal Tech columnist Joanna Stern  Joanna thanks for explaining this for us  anytime  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  head over to our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

